279 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 64 0 R
/T (bytes each that's only ~8 kbps, which an old dial-up modem could send. That's why SYN floods are so cheap. SYN cookies: when a SYN arrives the server doesn't store anything. It puts the connection details \(a hash of addresses/ports, a timestamp, the MSS, a secret\) into the initial sequence number of its SYN-ACK. Only if the client answers with an ACK that carries the right cookie + 1 does the server create the connection. Spoofed senders never answer, so the half-open queue never fills.)
/E (bytes each that's only ~8 kbps, which an old dial-up modem could send. That's why SYN floods are so cheap. SYN cookies: when a SYN arrives the server doesn't store anything. It puts the connection details \(a hash of addresses/ports, a timestamp, the MSS, a secret\) into the initial sequence number of its SYN-ACK. Only if the client answers with an ACK that carries the right cookie + 1 does the server create the connection. Spoofed senders never answer, so the half-open queue never fills.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
