1067 0 obj
<<
/Title (bytes each that's only ~8 kbps, which an old dial-up modem could send. That's why SYN floods are so cheap. SYN cookies: when a SYN arrives the server doesn't store anything. It puts the connection details \(a hash of addresses/ports, a timestamp, the MSS, a secret\) into the initial sequence number of its SYN-ACK. Only if the client answers with an ACK that carries the right cookie + 1 does the server create the connection. Spoofed senders never answer, so the half-open queue never fills.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
